Many polluted bays lack the surveys, tide and current records to calibrate
hydrodynamic models. We ask which conclusions about flushing survive without them.
Havana Bay (Cuba), a 4.9~km$^2$ pocket bay with a 1.5~km entrance channel, is modelled in
3D with SCHISM from public inputs only. Nine poorly known inputs, from
historical charts to river discharge, are sampled in a 704-member Sobol ensemble.
The tide alone exchanges little water; the density contrast of 2~m$^3$\,s$^{-1}$ of river
water does. Within the ensemble the bay-mean age is 7.8~days (5.6--10.4,
90\% range), never above 14~days; ageing from the channel to the inner coves is robust,
an age above one week is not. River discharge explains most of the variance and should be
measured first. The air--sea heat budget, not sampled, raises the age to at least 11~days:
the bay is renewed in one to two weeks, not months.
